{"task_id": "Mbpp/237", "solution": "def normalize_tuple(tuple_obj):\n    \"\"\"\n    Normalizes a tuple by sorting its elements in ascending order.\n    \"\"\"\n    return tuple(sorted(tuple_obj))\n\ndef count_occurrences(normalized_tuples):\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts occurrences of each unique normalized tuple.\n    \"\"\"\n    counts = {}\n    for tpl in normalized_tuples:\n        counts[tpl] = counts.get(tpl, 0) + 1\n    return counts\n\ndef check_occurences(tuple_list):\n    \"\"\"\n    Main function that counts occurrences of tuples treating order-equivalent tuples as same.\n    \"\"\"\n    normalized_list = []\n    for tpl in tuple_list:\n        normalized_list.append(normalize_tuple(tpl))\n    return count_occurrences(normalized_list)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/239", "solution": "def get_total_number_of_sequences(m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the number of sequences of length n, such that each element is a positive integer,\n    greater than or equal to twice the previous element, and less than or equal to m.\n    \"\"\"\n    # dp[i][j] stores the number of valid sequences of length i that end with the value j.\n    # The table is 1-indexed for both length (rows) and value (columns).\n    dp = [[0] * (m + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]\n\n    # Base case: For sequences of length 1.\n    # Any positive integer j from 1 to m can be a valid sequence of length 1.\n    for j in range(1, m + 1):\n        dp[1][j] = 1\n\n    # Fill the DP table for lengths from 2 up to n.\n    for i in range(2, n + 1):\n        # To efficiently calculate the sum of dp[i-1][k] for k in a range,\n        # we compute prefix sums for the (i-1)-th row.\n        # prefix_sum_prev_row[x] will store sum(dp[i-1][y] for y from 1 to x).\n        prefix_sum_prev_row = [0] * (m + 1)\n        for k_val in range(1, m + 1):\n            prefix_sum_prev_row[k_val] = prefix_sum_prev_row[k_val - 1] + dp[i - 1][k_val]\n\n        # For each possible ending value 'j_val' for a sequence of length 'i'.\n        for j_val in range(1, m + 1):\n            # A previous element 'k' must satisfy k <= j_val / 2.\n            # We sum counts for all valid previous elements k from 1 up to floor(j_val / 2).\n            max_prev_val_allowed = j_val // 2\n\n            # If there are valid previous elements (i.e., max_prev_val_allowed is at least 1),\n            # use the precomputed prefix sum. Otherwise, dp[i][j_val] remains 0.\n            if max_prev_val_allowed >= 1:\n                dp[i][j_val] = prefix_sum_prev_row[max_prev_val_allowed]\n\n    # The total number of sequences of length n is the sum of all counts in the dp[n] row.\n    total_sequences = sum(dp[n])\n\n    return total_sequences"}
