{"task_id": "Mbpp/245", "solution": "def max_sum(array: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum sum of a bitonic subsequence in the given array.\n    A bitonic sequence is first increasing and then decreasing.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(array)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    if n == 1:\n        return array[0]\n\n    # calculate_increasing_sums: dp_inc[i] stores max sum of increasing subsequence ending at array[i]\n    dp_inc = [0] * n\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp_inc[i] = array[i]\n        for j in range(i):\n            if array[j] < array[i]:\n                dp_inc[i] = max(dp_inc[i], dp_inc[j] + array[i])\n\n    # calculate_decreasing_sums: dp_dec[i] stores max sum of decreasing subsequence starting at array[i]\n    dp_dec = [0] * n\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        dp_dec[i] = array[i]\n        for j in range(n - 1, i, -1):\n            if array[j] < array[i]:\n                dp_dec[i] = max(dp_dec[i], dp_dec[j] + array[i])\n\n    max_bitonic_sum = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        # A bitonic subsequence must have at least one element.\n        # If dp_inc[i] or dp_dec[i] is just array[i], it means no valid increasing/decreasing part\n        # before/after array[i] was found *that extended a previous subsequence*.\n        # However, for a bitonic subsequence of length > 1, array[i] serves as the peak.\n        # The total sum would be (sum of increasing part ending at i) + (sum of decreasing part starting at i) - array[i] (to avoid double counting the peak).\n        # We also need to ensure that both an increasing part AND a decreasing part exist (even if trivial, i.e., just array[i])\n        # and that it's not a purely increasing or purely decreasing sequence, unless it has only 1 element.\n        # But for the problem, a \"bitonic subsequence\" can be purely increasing or purely decreasing (if it ends/starts at one end),\n        # but the common definition implies a peak.\n        # The design context example suggests a peak is involved.\n        # The condition dp_inc[i] > array[i] and dp_dec[i] > array[i] ensures that there is at least one element before\n        # and one element after 'i' that contribute to the sum, making it a \"proper\" bitonic sequence with a peak.\n        # If no such elements exist, dp_inc[i] and dp_dec[i] would just be array[i].\n        # Sum is dp_inc[i] + dp_dec[i] - array[i] to avoid double counting array[i] at the peak.\n        \n        # Consider the definition of bitonic: first increasing, then decreasing.\n        # This implicitly means it must have at least 2 elements to be truly \"increasing then decreasing\".\n        # If it's just increasing (e.g., 1, 5, 10), then it's a bitonic sequence where the decreasing part is empty.\n        # If it's just decreasing (e.g., 10, 5, 1), then it's a bitonic sequence where the increasing part is empty.\n        # But the common interpretation for \"max sum of bitonic subsequence\" implies a peak that contributes to both.\n        # If we take max(dp_inc[i] + dp_dec[i] - array[i]), this handles cases where there's no actual \"increase\" or \"decrease\"\n        # beyond the element itself, correctly falling back to just array[i] if only one part is valid.\n        # However, to be a valid bitonic subsequence with 'i' as a peak, it must have at least one element from the increasing\n        # part (j < i) and at least one element from the decreasing part (k > i), OR be a purely increasing/decreasing subsequence.\n        # The problem statement: \"first increasing and then decreasing\" implies there's a turning point.\n        # If it's a single element, it's trivial.\n        # If it's only increasing, e.g., [1, 5, 10], max sum = 16. If 10 is the peak, dp_inc[2]=16, dp_dec[2]=10. Sum = 16+10-10 = 16. Correct.\n        # If it's only decreasing, e.g., [10, 5, 1], max sum = 16. If 10 is the peak, dp_inc[0]=10, dp_dec[0]=16. Sum = 10+16-10 = 16. Correct.\n        # If it's bitonic, e.g., [1, 15, 51, 45, 33, 100, 12, 18, 9] -> peak 100.\n        # dp_inc for 100: [1, 15, 51, 45, 33, 100] -> sum = 1+15+33+100 or 1+15+51+100 (wrong, needs increasing, so 1+15+33+100 is not valid path).\n        # Let's re-evaluate dp_inc and dp_dec's meaning more precisely.\n        # dp_inc[i] is the max sum of an increasing subsequence ending at array[i].\n        # dp_dec[i] is the max sum of a decreasing subsequence starting at array[i].\n\n        # For a bitonic subsequence with peak at `array[i]`, its sum will be `dp_inc[i] + dp_dec[i] - array[i]`.\n        # This formula counts `array[i]` twice (once as the end of the increasing part, once as the start of the decreasing part).\n        # We need to ensure that the subsequence is not purely increasing or purely decreasing if `n > 1`.\n        # A subsequence must contain at least one element. If `n=1`, `array[0]` is the max sum.\n        # If `n > 1`, a bitonic subsequence could be purely increasing (e.g., `[1, 2, 3]`), or purely decreasing (e.g., `[3, 2, 1]`).\n        # In these cases, one of the parts (`dp_dec[i]` for purely increasing or `dp_inc[i]` for purely decreasing) would only be `array[i]`.\n        # Example: [1, 15, 51, 45, 33, 100, 12, 18, 9]\n        # Peak at 100 (index 5)\n        # Increasing part ending at 100: [1, 15, 51, 100] -> sum 167 (dp_inc[5] = 167)\n        # Decreasing part starting at 100: [100, 18, 9] -> sum 127 (dp_dec[5] = 127)\n        # Total sum = 167 + 127 - 100 = 194. This works.\n\n        # For a purely increasing sequence [1, 5, 10]:\n        # i=0: dp_inc[0]=1, dp_dec[0]=16 (10,5,1 reverse)\n        # i=1: dp_inc[1]=6, dp_dec[1]=15 (5,1 reverse)\n        # i=2: dp_inc[2]=16, dp_dec[2]=10\n        # If we take max(dp_inc[i] + dp_dec[i] - array[i]):\n        # i=0: 1 + 16 - 1 = 16\n        # i=1: 6 + 15 - 5 = 16\n        # i=2: 16 + 10 - 10 = 16\n        # Max is 16. This correctly handles purely increasing/decreasing.\n        \n        current_sum = dp_inc[i] + dp_dec[i] - array[i]\n        \n        # A bitonic subsequence must have at least two elements to be considered increasing then decreasing,\n        # unless it is implicitly a subsequence of length 1 (which would be array[i]).\n        # The problem example implies a non-trivial bitonic sequence.\n        # The calculation dp_inc[i] + dp_dec[i] - array[i] correctly computes the sum.\n        # We need to ensure that we consider only valid bitonic sequences.\n        # A bitonic sequence must have both an increasing part and a decreasing part,\n        # where either part can be trivial (just the peak element).\n        # For a sequence like [1, 2, 3], if 3 is the peak, dp_inc[2] = 6, dp_dec[2] = 3. Sum = 6+3-3 = 6.\n        # For a sequence like [3, 2, 1], if 3 is the peak, dp_inc[0] = 3, dp_dec[0] = 6. Sum = 3+6-3 = 6.\n        # The only invalid case is if both increasing and decreasing parts are trivial for an element.\n        # i.e., dp_inc[i] == array[i] and dp_dec[i] == array[i] for an element that is not an actual peak\n        # (e.g., if array[i] is smaller than all previous and smaller than all subsequent elements, it can't be a peak).\n        # However, the `max` operation will naturally select elements that actually form a larger sum.\n\n        # The key constraint is that a subsequence must be *first* increasing *then* decreasing.\n        # This implies it should have at least one element that is part of an increasing chain, and one part of a decreasing chain.\n        # If dp_inc[i] == array[i] (no actual increase before it) and dp_dec[i] == array[i] (no actual decrease after it),\n        # then array[i] is an isolated element that cannot form a proper bitonic subsequence of length > 1.\n        # Example: [5, 1, 10]. If '1' is the peak, dp_inc[1]=1, dp_dec[1]=1. Sum = 1+1-1=1. This is just element '1'.\n        # However, '5' could be a peak (dp_inc[0]=5, dp_dec[0]=5+1=6 if increasing is trivial and decreasing involves 1, or 5+10=15 if decreasing involves 10 - invalid).\n        # We are looking for the maximum sum, so this calculation `dp_inc[i] + dp_dec[i] - array[i]` is correct.\n        # It finds the sum if `array[i]` is considered the peak.\n        # If only increasing part exists, e.g., `[1, 2, 3]`, then for `i=2` (`array[i]=3`): `dp_inc[2]=6`, `dp_dec[2]=3`. Result `6+3-3=6`. Correct.\n        # If only decreasing part exists, e.g., `[3, 2, 1]`, then for `i=0` (`array[i]=3`): `dp_inc[0]=3`, `dp_dec[0]=6`. Result `3+6-3=6`. Correct.\n        # If `array[i]` is the single element chosen, e.g. `[10, 1, 20]`, for `i=1` (`array[i]=1`): `dp_inc[1]=1`, `dp_dec[1]=1`. Result `1+1-1=1`. Correct.\n\n        # The only edge case where we might need adjustment is if array[i] is chosen as the peak, but it cannot\n        # actually extend an increasing subsequence OR a decreasing subsequence (i.e. if it's not a valid peak in a non-trivial bitonic sequence).\n        # This would happen if dp_inc[i] == array[i] AND dp_dec[i] == array[i], meaning it is just array[i] itself.\n        # In this scenario, we only consider it if the overall `max_bitonic_sum` hasn't been initialized yet or is smaller.\n        # If n > 1, we only want to consider cases where it's truly bitonic (at least 2 elements).\n        # The sum will include `array[i]` itself.\n\n        # The logic for `dp_inc[i] + dp_dec[i] - array[i]` already ensures that if both `dp_inc[i]` and `dp_dec[i]` are just `array[i]`,\n        # the result is `array[i]`, which is the sum of a single-element subsequence.\n        # If `n == 0`, `max_bitonic_sum` would be 0.\n        # If `n == 1`, `dp_inc[0] = array[0]`, `dp_dec[0] = array[0]`. `max_bitonic_sum = array[0]`. This is correct.\n        # The problem asks for \"maximum sum of a bitonic subsequence\", not \"maximum sum of a bitonic subsequence of length > 1\".\n        # So a single element is a valid bitonic subsequence.\n        \n        # The solution will pick max(dp_inc[i] + dp_dec[i] - array[i]) for all i.\n        # This will cover all cases: purely increasing (peak at end), purely decreasing (peak at start),\n        # and truly bitonic (peak in middle).\n        \n        if current_sum > max_bitonic_sum:\n            max_bitonic_sum = current_sum\n    \n    # If the array has only one element, max_bitonic_sum will be initialized to 0.\n    # The loop will run once for i=0: dp_inc[0]=array[0], dp_dec[0]=array[0].\n    # current_sum = array[0] + array[0] - array[0] = array[0].\n    # max_bitonic_sum becomes array[0]. This is correct for n=1.\n\n    return max_bitonic_sum"}
